### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants a count of strings at each length. This tests the two-argument groupingBy with counting() as the downstream collector.

### How do you create a length histogram?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class LengthHistogram {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> codes = List.of(
            "SAV", "CUR", "SAV2001", "FD2024", "HDFC", "ICICI", "SBI");

        Map<Integer, Long> histogram = codes.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                String::length,
                TreeMap::new,
                Collectors.counting()));

        histogram.forEach((len, count) ->
            System.out.println("Length " + len + ": " + count + " codes"));
    }
}
```

```text
Length 3: 3 codes
Length 4: 1 codes
Length 5: 1 codes
Length 6: 1 codes
Length 7: 1 codes
```

### Why is counting() more efficient than grouping into lists?

```java
// Approach 1: group into lists, then get sizes
Map<Integer, List<String>> lists = codes.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length));
Map<Integer, Integer> sizes = new HashMap<>();
lists.forEach((k, v) -> sizes.put(k, v.size()));
// Stores every string in memory, then discards

// Approach 2: counting() directly
Map<Integer, Long> counts = codes.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length, Collectors.counting()));
// Never stores the strings, only increments counters
```

counting() is more efficient because it never materializes the group members.

### How do you swap the downstream for different aggregations?

| Downstream | Result type | What it computes |
|---|---|---|
| counting() | Long | Number of strings per length |
| summingInt(s -> 1) | Integer | Same as counting but as int |
| joining(", ") | String | Comma-separated strings per length |
| toSet() | Set<String> | Unique strings per length |

### How do you find the most common length?

```java
Optional<Map.Entry<Integer, Long>> mostCommon = histogram.entrySet()
    .stream()
    .max(Map.Entry.comparingByValue());

mostCommon.ifPresent(e ->
    System.out.println("Most common length: " + e.getKey()
        + " (" + e.getValue() + " strings)"));
```

```text
Most common length: 3 (3 strings)
```

### What about detecting anomalies?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class LengthAnomaly {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> codes = List.of(
            "SAV", "CUR", "SBI", "SAV2001", "XX");

        Map<Integer, Long> histogram = codes.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                String::length, TreeMap::new, Collectors.counting()));

        int expectedLength = 3;
        histogram.entrySet().stream()
            .filter(e -> e.getKey() != expectedLength)
            .forEach(e -> System.out.println(
                "ANOMALY: " + e.getValue() + " codes have length "
                + e.getKey() + " (expected " + expectedLength + ")"));
    }
}
```

```text
ANOMALY: 1 codes have length 2 (expected 3)
ANOMALY: 1 codes have length 7 (expected 3)
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: expecting Integer instead of Long from counting().

```java
// WRONG: counting() returns Long, not Integer
// Map<Integer, Integer> wrong = ...groupingBy(..., counting());
// compile error: incompatible types

// CORRECT: use Long
Map<Integer, Long> correct = codes.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(String::length, Collectors.counting()));
```

### What about visualizing the histogram?

```java
histogram.forEach((len, count) -> {
    String bar = "#".repeat(count.intValue());
    System.out.printf("Length %d: %s (%d)%n", len, bar, count);
});
```

```text
Length 3: ### (3)
Length 4: # (1)
Length 5: # (1)
Length 6: # (1)
Length 7: # (1)
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you get counts as Integer instead of Long?" Use summingInt(s -> 1) instead of counting().
- "How do you filter lengths with count above a threshold?" Stream the entrySet and filter by value.
- "How do you create a character frequency histogram?" Same pattern with chars() and groupingBy(c -> c, counting()).
